---
// ── SEO COPY REWRITE APPLIED, 16 September 2026 ───────────────────
//
// This page has been rewritten against construction-site-cleaning-copy-rewrite.md
// (workbook row 30, primary `post construction cleaning services` 150/mo,
// cluster 390/mo). Section numbers in the comments below are that brief's.
//
// THE PROBLEM THE BRIEF NAMES, in its own words: the writing on this page is
// the best in the engagement and the keyword targeting was the worst. The
// mapped primary is `post construction cleaning services` and the word "post"
// appeared nowhere in the title, the H1, the URL, the opening paragraph or
// the meta. The page speaks builder, which is right, and the workbook targets
// the phrase a builder types into Google, which is also right. They just
// never met. The changes below fix that WITHOUT touching the builder
// language, which stays in the kicker, the demoted headline and the body.
//
// Implemented: the approved title and meta, a new H1 carrying the mapped
// phrase with the old one demoted into the hero lead, the mapped phrase in
// the opening paragraph, the approved CTA in all five places, the renamed FAQ
// headings and the full-footprint town list.
//
// 🔴 ONE BLOCKING CHANGE REQUEST IS OPEN AND SHIPS UNRESOLVED. The approved
// CTA, "Book your clean", contradicts the page it sits on: §11 says a
// builders clean is three jobs at three moments and which ones you need has
// to be settled first, the booking section says commercial work is priced on
// the site, non-branded FAQ 1 says a phone quote for a builders clean is
// usually wrong, and BFD §13.2 rules out pricing this class of work sight
// unseen. It is PLACED, per the brief's Rule 4, and it SHOULD NOT GO LIVE
// until the workbook owner rules on the wording. Suggested replacement, and
// it is the page's own closing heading: "Tell us your handover date." See
// `walkthroughCta` below.
//
// TWO FURTHER BLOCKING ITEMS, both blocked on the client rather than on copy,
// and both still live as brackets in the copy below:
//   🔴 Does TLB own an H-class vacuum? Stage two asks it of itself. §3 tells
//     the reader to ask every cleaner which filtration they are bringing, so
//     a page that raises the question and cannot answer it invites a reader
//     to disqualify TLB. If the answer is no, §3 must be reworded before
//     anything ships.
//   🔴 Does every attending cleaner hold a current white card, and can TLB
//     provide a SWMS? Branded FAQ 1 asks it of itself and its own bracket
//     says "confirm before this page ships". That is correct.
// Both are why no comparison table is supplied here: the rows a construction
// table needs are white card, SWMS, H-class equipment and insurance, and
// every one of them is currently an unconfirmed bracket. Writing them now
// would mean writing claims TLB cannot stand behind, which BFD §2.4 rules
// out. Build it once those two are answered and it will be the strongest
// table on the site.
//
// Not done here, because they are not copy: the URL mismatch (the workbook
// wants /commercial-cleaning/construction-site-cleaning; the live slug
// contains no form of the word cleaning at all, which the brief calls the
// worst URL in the engagement — a redirect decision for the build team), the
// canonical domain, FAQ schema, the stage one image reusing the hero frame,
// the header's competing "Get an instant quote", and hiding the testimonial
// block until real quotes exist.
//
// Construction site cleaning — one of the THIRTEEN "by type of premises" leaf
// pages under the /commercial-cleaning/ hub (navigation.ts, "By type of
// premises" group). Built in one pass with its twelve siblings.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS for any of the thirteen. All copy below is DRAFTED,
// not supplied. See src/pages/commercial-cleaning/office-cleaning.astro's header for the launch
// blockers shared across the whole cluster.
//
// ✅ SLUG RESOLVED 17 Sep 2026. This page used to sit at /construction-site/
// — the literal menu label, and a phrase nobody searches. The client's URL
// sheet settles it as /commercial-cleaning/construction-site-cleaning/, which
// carries one of the two head terms ("construction cleaning"; the other is
// "builders clean"). navigation.ts still LABELS the row "Construction site",
// which is that sheet's own label/slug pairing and not a drift to fix.
// The TITLE and the H1 still lead with "builders clean", which is where the
// other head term matters most for search.
//
// WHAT MAKES THIS PAGE DIFFERENT from its twelve siblings, structurally:
//   - The scope is a SEQUENCE, not a set. Three cleans happen at three
//     points in a build and they are different jobs, so §4 is
//     `ServiceBlocks variant="slider"` — one slide per stage, moved through
//     in order. The only use of that variant in the cluster, and the only
//     page whose content has an inherent order.
//   - `Callout tone="warning"` on silica dust, and it is the most serious
//     warning on the whole site. Not softened.
//   - `MetricsBlock variant="list"` is deliberately NOT used and no numbers
//     appear anywhere: this page has no honest figures and inventing a
//     "500+ sites cleaned" is exactly what the brand foundation document's
//     differentiator 2 argues against.
//   - No `ComparisonTable`. The shared rows are written for someone choosing
//     who enters their home; a site supervisor is choosing a subcontractor
//     who can hit a handover date. Deliberate omission, same call as strata.
//
// BLOCKS LAUNCH, specific to this page:
//   - IS THIS SAFE FOR TLB TO OFFER? This is the one page in the cluster
//     where the answer might be no. A builders clean is construction work on
//     a construction site: white card induction, site-specific induction,
//     SWMS, silica exposure, working near live trades, and often working at
//     height. Confirm TLB has the tickets, the equipment and the insurance
//     before this page ships. If the honest answer is "we do the final
//     detail clean after handover but not builders cleans", then §4's first
//     two slides come off and this page becomes a much shorter one.
//   - Whether TLB holds a white card for every cleaner who would attend.
//     Bracketed in branded FAQ 1. Without it, no builder can lawfully let
//     them on site.
//   - Whether TLB has an H-class vacuum. Bracketed in §4 stage two. An
//     ordinary vacuum on construction dust is both useless and a health
//     issue, and this is the equipment question a builder will ask.
//   - Waste removal. Builders routinely expect the cleaner to take rubbish
//     away. Bracketed in §4 stage one.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import ContentGrid from '../../components/sections/ContentGrid.astro';
import ImageBand from '../../components/sections/ImageBand.astro';
import TagCloud from '../../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, googleReviews } from '../../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own frames are in src/assets/commercial_cleaning/premises/;
// the prompts are in that folder's IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
//
// ⚠️ THE HI-VIS EXCEPTION. IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 sets the canonical uniform as
// aqua polo, dark teal apron and teal gloves, and the site's palette rule
// forbids yellow. All three frames on this page break that rule on purpose:
// a cleaner on a construction site wears a high-visibility vest, and
// fluorescent lime-green is the standard Australian hi-vis colour and the
// closest real one to the brand's Mint Green. A frame of a cleaner in an
// apron on a live site would be a photo of something that must not happen.
// The apron is dropped for the same reason. Documented in the folder's
// IMAGE-PROMPTS.md so this does not get "corrected" back later.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as the hub.
import footerLogoImg from '../../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../../data/navigation';
import { footprintLocationGroups, footprintSubheading } from '../../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../../data/trust';
import { commercialBusiness, relatedPremises, walkthroughCtaLabel } from '../../data/premises';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../../data/brand-lines';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../../lib/cms-schemas';

// §0 Metadata. "Builders clean" leads the title because it is the head term,
// even though the slug is the menu's literal label. See the file header.
//
// In `pageMeta`, on "Post Construction Cleaning Services | TLB Cleaning":
// Brief §2. The approved title, verbatim, at 49 characters. It carries
// `post construction cleaning services` 150/mo contiguously; the live one
// carried neither that phrase nor `construction site cleaning services`,
// because the word "and" broke both.
//
// ⚠️ CHANGE REQUEST for the workbook owner, brief §2: the approved title
// drops "builders clean" entirely, and that is the phrase a Northern Rivers
// builder actually says. The live SERP on 16 September 2026 showed
// Australian competitors running both phrases together, which is what the
// live title was doing, and there are nine spare characters. Pasted as
// approved per Rule 3.
// In `pageMeta`, on `description`:
// Brief §3. The approved meta, verbatim, at 143 characters. The live one was
// 167 and would have been cut off. This opens on the mapped phrase, picks up
// `post renovation cleaning` and names the stage range, which is the page's
// own framing.
//
// 🔴 The blocking CTA below is the last sentence of this meta too. What is
// lost either way: the live meta said "Booked to your handover date", which
// is this page's single strongest promise and the thing a site supervisor
// actually clicks. If the workbook owner is revisiting the CTA anyway, that
// is the wording to return to. The H1 rescues the line in the meantime.
// In `pageMeta`, on `canonical`:
// ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.

// §7 The primary CTA, defined once and repeated in five places per BFD §9.5:
// the hero button, each of the three stage slides and the closing block.
//
// 🔴 BLOCKING — DO NOT PUBLISH WITHOUT A RULING. See the file header. The
// label below is the workbook's approved CTA, placed exactly as specified per
// the brief's Rule 4, and it promises a transaction this page spends 1,200
// words explaining is not available yet. The conflict is at its sharpest in
// the closing block, where the heading says "Tell us your handover date." and
// the button beneath it says "Book your clean" on a page that has just
// explained why the clean cannot be booked until the stages are settled.
// Suggested replacement for the workbook: "Tell us your handover date."

// §1 Hero. `split-single-image`, image right, matching the rest of the
// commercial cluster.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED, and note the hi-vis exception above.
//
// In `hero`, on "Builders clean and construction site cleaning":
// Brief §8. The kicker does not change. With the H1 now carrying the mapped
// phrase, this is where the builder's own vocabulary lives, and keeping both
// is how the page serves the search term and the reader at the same time.
// In `hero`, on `headingLines`:
// Brief §4. The exact phrase `post construction cleaning services` is worth
// 15 points scored present or absent, and the handover extension is the
// page's real promise rather than decoration — it also rescues the line the
// approved meta drops.
// In `hero`, on `lead`:
// Brief §5 and §6, in that order. The demoted H1 opens the lead — it is the
// best opening line on the site, it speaks directly to a builder's standing
// with a client, and it survives the move intact — and the hero paragraph
// follows. Hero has no separate subheading slot, so the two run as one lead.
//
// Every word of the builder language is kept, moved into sentence three.

// §2 Definition. Answer-target block, and it has to do double duty: define
// the term and separate the three stages, because "builders clean" means a
// different thing to a builder, a project manager and a homeowner.

// §3 Silica. `warning`, and THE MOST SERIOUS WARNING ON THIS SITE.
//
// Placed before the scope, same reasoning as the clinical waste callout on
// the medical page: what a contractor refuses to do badly is the thing a
// competent site supervisor screens for first.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED, and take this one seriously:
//   - Respirable crystalline silica is generated by cutting, grinding and
//     sweeping concrete, brick, tile, render and engineered stone, and dry
//     sweeping is a recognised way of putting settled dust back into the air.
//     Correct.
//   - Australia banned the manufacture, supply and installation of
//     engineered stone benchtops from 1 July 2024. Correct as at this build,
//     and the reason the paragraph names engineered stone.
//   - Safe Work Australia and the state regulators set exposure standards and
//     control requirements. Correct. NO EXPOSURE FIGURE IS QUOTED on purpose:
//     the workplace exposure standard for respirable crystalline silica has
//     been reduced within recent memory and a stale number here would be
//     both wrong and dangerous.
//   - The brand foundation document (§3.3) names Safe Work Australia as an
//     approved authoritative source, so a link belongs here at launch.

// §4 The three stages. `ServiceBlocks variant="slider"` — THE ONLY USE OF
// THIS VARIANT IN THE CLUSTER, and it is here because these three genuinely
// happen in order. Prev/next through a sequence is what the variant is for
// (see SECTIONS.md: navigation, not selection — the medical page's three
// settings are a selection and use `highlight` instead).
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against standard practice, not supplied by TLB.
// Two of the three carry real brackets, and both are equipment or licensing
// questions rather than copy questions.
//
// In `stageItems`, on "Stage one: progress clean, while the trades are still in" (description):
// ⚠️ Real bracket. Builders routinely expect the cleaner to remove
// rubbish, and that needs a vehicle, a tip account and a price.
// In `stageItems`, on "Stage two: the builders clean, once the trades are out" (description):
// ⚠️ Real bracket, and it is the equipment question a builder asks.

// §5 What decides the price. `ContentGrid` with `feature` cells. Included
// because this is the premises type where a quote most often turns out to
// mean something different from what the builder assumed, and naming the
// variables is more useful than a number we do not have.

// §6 "Worth knowing". The lever on this page is the defect list: a builders
// clean is the only trade whose work is judged by someone actively looking
// for faults, and dust is what gets written up.

// §7 Related pages. Real estate and end of lease are appended as extras: a
// builder finishing a spec home and an agent presenting it are the same
// handover from two sides, and the reader is often both.

// §9 Branded FAQs, in the CUSTOMER'S voice — a builder, supervisor, project
// manager or a homeowner mid-renovation.
//
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Are your cleaners inducted to work on site?" (answer):
// ⚠️ THE page's gate, along with the H-class vacuum. See the file
// header. Without white cards, no builder can lawfully let TLB on site.
// In `brandedFaqItems`, on "Do you take the rubbish away?" (answer):
// ⚠️ Same bracket as §4 stage one.

// §10 Non-branded FAQs, the long-tail block.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS REQUIRED. Checkable claims:
//   - A general construction induction card (white card) is required to carry
//     out construction work in NSW. Correct.
//   - Dry sweeping is a recognised silica exposure risk and wet methods plus
//     H-class vacuums are the standard controls. Correct; no exposure figure
//     quoted, on purpose.
//   - "Builders clean" has no legal or standard definition, which is why the
//     answer below tells the reader to define it in writing. Correct and
//     genuinely useful.
//   - No dollar figure anywhere, same as every sibling page.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "How much does a builders clean cost?" (answer):
// ⚠️ BRACKETED ON PURPOSE, same as every sibling page.

// §11 Closing CTA.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/commercial-cleaning/construction-site-cleaning.yaml
// and is edited in Pages CMS ("Construction site cleaning"). This file owns the layout. The
// notes above explain why each section says what it says; they stay here
// because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
const page = await getPage(
  'commercial-cleaning/construction-site-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    // The label of the page's main button. It links to the quote page in Site
    // settings. Empty falls back to the site-wide walkthrough label.
    ctaLabel: section.optional(section.text),
    hero: section.hero,
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    silica: section.callout,
    // A stage with no button of its own gets the page's main button.
    stages: section.imageCards,
    detailPhoto: section.imageBand,
    priceDrivers: section.contentGrid,
    worthKnowing: section.textBlock,
    // The pills are the other premises pages, from Site settings, plus this
    // page's own extra links (`tags`), which go in after them (§7 above).
    related: section.tagCloud.extend({ note: section.optional(z.array(section.text)) }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    // The subheading falls back to the shared footprint line, and the towns
    // come from Site settings.
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud.omit({ tags: true }).extend({ subheading: section.optional(section.text) }),
    // Heading only. The paragraphs are the shared brand lines.
    brandLine: section.sectionHeading,
    brandedFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/commercial-cleaning/construction-site-cleaning/',
};

const walkthroughCta = { label: page.ctaLabel ?? walkthroughCtaLabel, href: quoteCta.href };

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '16/9');

// Stage one reuses the 16:9 hero frame; the other two are 4:3 sources.
const stageItems = page.stages.items.map((item, i) => ({
  ...item,
  image: item.image && sized(item.image, i === 0 ? '16/9' : '4/3'),
  cta: item.cta ?? walkthroughCta,
}));

const priceDriverBlocks = page.priceDrivers.items.map((item) =>
  item.type === 'image' ? { type: 'image' as const, ...sized(item.image, '4/3') } : item,
);

const relatedTags = relatedPremises('/commercial-cleaning/construction-site-cleaning/', page.related.tags ?? []);

const business = commercialBusiness(pageMeta.description);
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    <Backdrop image={page.definition.backgroundImage} tint="muted" opacity={90}>
      <TrustBar variant="card" cards={trustBarCards} />
      <TextBlock
        theme="muted"
        align="left"
        heading={page.definition.heading}
        body={page.definition.body}
        cta={page.definition.cta}
      />
    </Backdrop>

    {/* §3 — before the scope. The most serious warning on the site, and the
        thing a competent supervisor screens a cleaner for first. */}
    <Callout tone="warning" heading={page.silica.heading} body={page.silica.body} cta={page.silica.cta} />

    {/* §4 — the only `slider` in the cluster. Three stages that genuinely
        happen in order, so navigation through a sequence rather than a
        selection between alternatives. See SECTIONS.md on the distinction. */}
    <ServiceBlocks
      variant="slider"
      heading={page.stages.heading}
      lead={page.stages.lead}
      tileCtaLabel={page.stages.tileCtaLabel}
      items={stageItems}
    />

    {/* The detail-clean frame, given a band of its own because it is the one
        photo on the page showing the OUTCOME rather than the work, and the
        outcome is what a handover is judged on.

        ⚠️ Source is 4:3 in a 16/9 box, so `cover` crops top and bottom.
        Checked: the vanity, the tapware and the screen all survive it. */}
    <ImageBand
      ratio="16/9"
      label={page.detailPhoto.image.label}
      caption={page.detailPhoto.caption}
      src={page.detailPhoto.image.src}
    />

    <ContentGrid heading={page.priceDrivers.heading} lead={page.priceDrivers.lead} columns={2} blocks={priceDriverBlocks} />

    <TextBlock
      theme="dark"
      align="left"
      heading={page.worthKnowing.heading}
      body={page.worthKnowing.body}
      cta={page.worthKnowing.cta}
      backgroundImage={page.worthKnowing.backgroundImage}
    />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.related.heading}
      subheading={page.related.subheading}
      tags={relatedTags}
      note={page.related.note ?? []}
    />

    <GoogleReviewSlider heading={page.reviews.heading} reviews={googleReviews} profileUrl={googleBusinessProfileUrl} />

    <TagCloud
      heading={page.whereWeClean.heading}
      subheading={page.whereWeClean.subheading ?? footprintSubheading}
      groups={footprintLocationGroups}
      note={page.whereWeClean.note}
    />

    {/* §1.3 The Line as a section heading, with §1.4 as its standfirst.
        The copy, and the reason this page gets `fast` rather than the other
        variant, both live in data/brand-lines.ts. Change it there, not here.

        `muted`, not `dark`: TagCloud above paints --color-brand and Faq below
        paints --color-surface, so Cream between them keeps the page
        alternating instead of stacking two dark bands.

        No CTA prop. §9.5 wants one primary CTA per page, repeated, and the
        closing CallToAction is directly under the FAQs. */}
    <TextBlock
      theme="muted"
      align="left"
      heading={page.brandLine.heading}
      body={[managedForYou.recurring, ...theLineBody.commercial, ...howItWorks.general]}
    />

    {/* Brief §24 and §25. The page carries two FAQ headings and the generic
        one made the split look like a build error: this block is about
        working with TLB on your build, the next is about the service. The
        second heading also lands the mapped phrase contiguously, which is
        the 10-point H2 component the page previously half scored, and drops
        Title Case, which BFD §9.2 bans. */}
    <Faq heading={page.brandedFaq.heading} lead={page.brandedFaq.lead} items={page.brandedFaq.items} />
    <Faq heading={page.faq.heading} lead={page.faq.lead} items={page.faq.items} />

    <CallToAction
      variant="secondary"
      kicker={page.closingCta.kicker}
      heading={page.closingCta.heading}
      lead={page.closingCta.lead}
      cta={page.closingCta.cta ?? walkthroughCta}
    />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: footerLogoImg }}
    columns={[
      { title: 'Services', links: footerServiceLinks },
      { title: 'Locations', links: [{ label: 'View all locations', href: '/locations/' }] },
    ]}
    contact={footerContact}
    copyright={footerCopyright}
  />
</Base>
